WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.895 --> 00:00:15.970
OLIVIA: Welcome to another edition of People in the News ! This week Ben will be telling us about

2
00:00:15.971 --> 00:00:20.703
the British writer J. K. Rowling. She's the bestselling author of an extremely popular book series for children and

3
00:00:20.704 --> 00:00:27.862
adults—Harry Potter! BEN: Thank you, Olivia. Joanne, or Jo, Rowling was born in England on July 31, 1965. As a

4
00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:41.130
young girl, Jo loved to use her imagination and invent stories. She often turned these stories into games in

5
00:00:41.131 --> 00:00:47.263
which she and her sister played the roles of different characters. One of the first stories Jo remembers writing

6
00:00:47.264 --> 00:00:55.424
was about a character named Rabbit who got sick and went to visit his friend, Miss Bee. After college

7
00:00:55.425 --> 00:01:04.591
Jo worked for Amnesty International in London, where she read many letters written by people who had suffered terrible

8
00:01:04.592 --> 00:01:12.170
human rights abuses. Their stories affected her deeply, making her painfully aware of the evil things that people are capable

9
00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:19.305
of doing to other people. Later she would describe her job at Amnesty International as one of the most

10
00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:25.327
inspiring and humbling experiences of her life. Even though Jo wasn't yet writing books professionally, she was constantly coming

11
00:01:25.328 --> 00:01:34.725
up with story ideas. Then one day in 1990, during a long train ride, Jo got the idea for

12
00:01:34.726 --> 00:01:42.719
a character named Harry Potter, an orphan who discovers he's a wizard. The train was delayed for four hours,

13
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.725
and she spent the whole time imagining what Harry was like and what sort of adventures he could have. Unfortunately,

14
00:01:48.726 --> 00:01:53.961
though, Jo didn't have a pen with her, and she was too shy to ask anybody for one. So

15
00:01:53.962 --> 00:01:59.284
she had to keep all her ideas in her head until she got home and could write everything down.

16
00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:11.425
Over the next five years, Jo worked on developing her ideas for the Harry Potter books. She filled countless

17
00:02:11.426 --> 00:02:20.275
notebooks with details about characters, Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry, magic spells and potions, and, of course, Voldemort,

18
00:02:20.276 --> 00:02:26.512
the evil wizard who killed Harry's parents. Jo also outlined plots for seven books, and she began working on the

19
00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:30.430
first book in the series, Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone . This might surprise some people, but Jo's

20
00:02:30.431 --> 00:02:36.253
life was not easy while she was writing the first Harry Potter book. Her mother died, and she got

21
00:02:36.254 --> 00:02:45.747
married and divorced. By 1993 Jo was a single mother living on public assistance, and she was very poor.

22
00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:52.071
Jo eventually found a job teaching. She knew once the job started, she would have little time to write,

23
00:02:52.072 --> 00:03:00.992
so she was determined to finish the first book before then. With her daughter sleeping in a stroller by her

24
00:03:00.993 --> 00:03:05.728
side, Jo spent many hours writing in a local cafe. Finally in 1995 the manuscript for Harry Potter and

25
00:03:05.729 --> 00:03:15.056
the Philosopher’s Stone was done, and Jo set out to find a publisher for it. OLIVIA: How did that

26
00:03:15.057 --> 00:03:19.639
go? Did the book get published right away? BEN: Nope. It's hard to believe, but Harry Potter and the

27
00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:25.947
Philosopher's Stone was rejected by 12 different publishers! Most of them said the book was too long, and that

28
00:03:25.948 --> 00:03:29.714
kids wouldn't want to read it. Eventually a small publisher accepted the book, but even there, editors didn't think it

29
00:03:29.715 --> 00:03:34.464
would sell well. OLIVIA: Really? Then why did they publish it? BEN: Because the daughter of the company's CEO

30
00:03:34.465 --> 00:03:38.381
read the manuscript and loved it. And it's a good thing the adults at the company trusted her opinion.

31
00:03:38.382 --> 00:03:45.698
Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone got great reviews, won several awards, and sold really well. In 1998 an

32
00:03:45.699 --> 00:03:57.863
American publisher paid Jo a huge amount of money to publish Harry Potter in the U.S.—so much money, in

33
00:03:57.864 --> 00:04:02.851
fact, that Jo was able to quit teaching and start writing full-time. As everybody knows, Harry Potter soon became an

34
00:04:02.852 --> 00:04:11.925
international phenomenon. The final book, Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows , was released in 2007 and became one

35
00:04:11.926 --> 00:04:16.931
of the fastest-selling books of all time. Many elements in the Harry Potter books were inspired by things from

36
00:04:16.932 --> 00:04:21.760
Jo's real life. For instance, she based Harry’s friend Hermione on herself as a young girl. Jo's loss of

37
00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:27.075
her own mother affected how she wrote about the death of Harry's parents. The train Hogwarts Express plays an

38
00:04:27.076 --> 00:04:34.679
important role in the series because Jo got the original idea for Harry Potter on a train. And the series

39
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:41.742
highlights the fight between good and evil, reflecting the influence of Jo's job at Amnesty International. In 2001 Harry

40
00:04:41.743 --> 00:04:49.154
Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone , which is the American title of the first book, was made into a

41
00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:54.986
movie. It was a huge hit in theaters, and each book in the series has since been made into

42
00:04:54.987 --> 00:04:59.133
a movie as well. Jo was able to retain a certain level of control with the films. The scripts

43
00:04:59.134 --> 00:05:06.474
all had to be approved by her, and when it came time to cast actors, she insisted they all be

44
00:05:06.475 --> 00:05:12.808
British. In addition to the books and films, there are Harry Potter toys, video games, and even a theme

45
00:05:12.809 --> 00:05:16.629
park! There would be still more Harry Potter merchandise if Jo allowed it. She has been approached by many

46
00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:22.864
companies wanting to use Harry in advertisements, and Jo has turned most of them down. And, Olivia, did you

47
00:05:22.865 --> 00:05:27.949
know that someone wanted to create a Harry Potter musical? We'll never know what that would have been like

48
00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:32.541
because Jo said no way! OLIVIA: It sounds like it was a struggle to get the first Harry Potter book

49
00:05:32.542 --> 00:05:42.521
published, but I’m sure glad it finally found its way into print. I bet Jo is glad about that

50
00:05:42.522 --> 00:05:53.233
too! Thanks for telling us all about this popular author, Ben. We'll see you next week, Little Fox readers!

